// Regression: a goroutine parked on a fiber-suspending host call
// (window.active -> runBlocking -> EXTERNAL wait) must keep making
// progress while the pump idles between ticks.
//
// what: go fn ticker() sleeps 50ms and counts; go fn poller() calls
//   window.active() in a loop (each call parks the goroutine on an
//   EXTERNAL wait) and counts. After 1.5s both counters are read.
// why: hasPendingWork() only checked runnable fibers, deferred actions
//   and DUE sleepers. A goroutine parked on an EXTERNAL wait is none of
//   those — and the tick that would drain its ASYNC_HOST_COMPLETE event
//   was skipped by the idle probe, so the completion event sat in the
//   queue and the goroutine never woke. Observed in the user's hotkey
//   config: update() (window.active loop) froze; every other parked
//   goroutine froze; interval timers (EventQueue-driven) kept running;
//   hotkeys kept firing. Fix: hasPendingWork also reports event-driven
//   awaiters and pending queue events, and tickGoroutines/tickScheduler
//   sweep abandoned completions.
// expected: ticks > 0, polls > 0, PASS. Before the fix: polls stalls at
//   0-1 while ticks advance (or both stall once the poller parks), FAIL.
// Runs headless: window.active() on the null-display path returns a
// null/info-valid=false object through the same park/resume machinery,
// which is exactly the machinery under test.

ticks = 0
polls = 0

go fn ticker() {
  loop {
    sleep(50)
    ticks = ticks + 1
  }
}

go fn poller() {
  loop {
    w = window.active()
    polls = polls + 1
    sleep(10)
  }
}

sleep(1500)

print("ticks: $ticks polls: $polls")
if ticks < 10 {
  print("FAIL: ticker goroutine starved (ticks=$ticks, expected >= 10)")
  exit(1)
}
if polls < 10 {
  print("FAIL: poller goroutine starved (polls=$polls, expected >= 10)")
  exit(1)
}
print("PASS: park/resume pump progress (ticks=$ticks polls=$polls)")
exit(0)
